course: everyday
lesson: 522
title: Adoption
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Adoption is when a child whose birth parents can't bring them up joins a new family for good. The birth parents are the ones the child was born to; the adoptive parents are the ones who bring the child up. Both are real, in different ways. Adoption is made official by a judge in a family court. On Romilly's court day she wears her sparkly shoes. The judge smiles, signs the papers and says, «Congratulations.» Then he gives her a teddy from a basket. «Proper job!» says Grandad (West Country for well done, great). Romilly names the teddy Judge.
explain: Isla always knew she was adopted; her mum and dad told her when she was tiny, with a picture book about a family. Now she's nine and asks, «What was my birth mum like?» They don't know everything, but they show her a letter her birth mum wrote when Isla was a baby. Mila gets a letter from her birth mum once a year, passed on through the adoption agency; this is called letterbox contact. Mila keeps every letter in a shoebox with a ribbon round it. Kiran wonders if his birth dad has the same nose and the same laugh. His mum says that when he's grown up, he can usually find out more.
explain: Some children find out late, and that's hard. Callum learns at nine, from his cousin at a party: «Ye're adopted, did ye no' know?» («no'» is Scots for not). He's shocked and furious, and storms home. His parents sit with him and admit they kept waiting for «the right time», which never came. «We should've told you years ago. We're sorry. Ask us anything.» It takes Callum a long time to feel okay. Most families now tell children early, so it's never a shock.
explain: Adopted children hear all sorts. A kid tells Marcus-Lee, «Your mom's not your REAL mom, that's facts» («facts» means that's true). Marcus-Lee says, «She's real. She's the one who's here.» Ana was born in Colombia and looks different from her mum and dad. In Boston people stare at them in the supermarket. «It's wicked annoying,» Ana says («wicked» means very, in New England). «Yeah, I'm adopted, it's not a big deal.» She answers when she wants to, and doesn't when she doesn't.
explain: Families made by adoption come in many shapes. Sam-Jo and his big sister Ruby were adopted together, so they never had to be apart; they're close, and they fight over the remote like any other brother and sister. Theo's sister Priya was adopted too, from a different birth family. «We're both adopted, so we're basically twins,» she jokes. They're four years apart. And every year on the day Zac's adoption became official, his family has pizza and a film. «Gotcha day!» he shouts. Some families call it «family day» instead, because some adopted people don't like the word «gotcha». Either way, it's a celebration of becoming a family.
fact: Adoption is when a child joins a new family permanently because their birth parents can't bring them up.
fact: Birth parents are the parents a child was born to; adoptive parents are the parents who bring the child up.
fact: Adoptive parents are real parents.
fact: An adoption is made official by a judge in a family court.
fact: Many families tell children they are adopted from very early on, so it is never a shock; finding out late can be very hard.
fact: Adopted children are often curious about their birth family, and that is normal.
fact: Letterbox contact means letters between an adopted child's family and the birth family, passed on through an agency.
fact: When adopted people grow up, they can usually find out more about their birth family.
fact: Adoptive families can look different from each other; staring is rude, and a child chooses what to share.
fact: Brothers and sisters are sometimes adopted together so they can stay together.
fact: Some families celebrate the day an adoption became official, sometimes called «Gotcha day» or «family day».
fact: «Proper job» is West Country for well done, «no'» is Scots for not, «facts» means that's true, and «wicked» in New England means very.
exercise: What is adoption? | A child joining a new family for good | A child staying with carers for a short time | A child visiting cousins for the summer | A child going to boarding school far away
exercise: Who are birth parents? | The parents a child was born to | The parents who throw the birthday party | The parents who bring a child up | The doctors at the hospital who help
exercise: Who makes an adoption official? | A judge in a family court | The child's head teacher at school | The police at the station | The birth parents' neighbours
exercise: What did Romilly get from the judge? | A teddy | A medal and a certificate | A new bike from the court | A letter telling her off
exercise: «Proper job!» says Grandad. What does it mean? | Well done, great | Go and get a job | That's a real job | Do it properly
exercise: How did Isla find out she was adopted? | Her parents told her when she was tiny | A cousin told her at a party | She read it on the internet one night | She found out at nine
exercise: Isla asks about her birth mum. Is that normal? | Yes, adopted children are often curious | No, it means she doesn't love her parents | No, adopted children never wonder about it | Yes, but only if she asks in secret
exercise: What is letterbox contact? | Letters passed on through an agency | Posting a letter to the judge | A special letterbox shaped like a little house | Texting a birth parent every day
exercise: Where does Mila keep her letters? | In a shoebox with a ribbon | In the bin after reading | Under the doormat by the front door | On her bedroom wall
exercise: Kiran wonders if his birth dad looks like him. What did his mum say? | That when he's grown up, he can usually find out more | That he must never, ever wonder about it again | That he looks exactly like his birth dad, she knows it | That birth dads are not allowed to be talked about
exercise: Callum found out at nine from his cousin. How did he feel? | Shocked and furious | Pleased and calm | Not bothered at all | Sleepy and bored
exercise: What did Callum's parents say? | They should've told him years ago, and he could ask anything | That his cousin had made it all up to upset him | That he should forget about it and never ask again | That it was really his cousin's job to tell him, not theirs at all
exercise: «Your mom's not your REAL mom.» What did Marcus-Lee answer? | «She's real. She's the one who's here» | «You're right, she's only pretend» | «My real mom is a famous singer in LA, actually» | Nothing, he cried all day
exercise: Ana says the staring is «wicked annoying». What does «wicked» mean here? | Very | Evil | Funny | Hardly
exercise: Why were Sam-Jo and Ruby adopted together? | So they never had to be apart | Because Ruby asked for a brother | Because the judge picked them at random | Because they were twins born the same day
exercise: Priya says she and Theo are «basically twins». Are they? | No, it's a joke; they're four years apart | Yes, they were born on the same day | Yes, they have the same birth mum | No, they're cousins really, from the same town
exercise: Why do some families say «family day» instead of «Gotcha day»? | Some adopted people don't like the word «gotcha» | It is against the law to say «gotcha» | Because «family day» is the only name a judge allows | Because «gotcha» is a word only for dogs
test: «adopted kids don't have real parents». Right? | No, adoptive parents are real parents | Yes, only birth parents are real | Yes, until they turn 18 | No, because they're all orphans with no parents
test: What would most likely happen if Callum's parents had told him when he was tiny? | It wouldn't have been such a shock | He would have been even angrier at nine | His cousin would never have known | He would never be curious at all
test: In a cartoon, an adopted kid finds their «real» rich family and leaves the adoptive one. What's more real? | Adoption is permanent; curiosity doesn't mean leaving | All adopted kids secretly go back to birth parents | Birth families are always rich and famous | Adopted children are allowed to swap families
test: Somebody stares at Ana and her mum in the supermarket. What is true? | Staring is rude, and Ana chooses what to share | Ana must explain her whole story to everyone | Ana's mum should carry the adoption papers | It's polite to stare at families who look different
test: «u wonder about ur birth dad?? so u don't love ur dad then». What's true? | Wondering is normal and doesn't take love away | Wondering means you love your dad less | You must choose which one of your two dads to love | Adopted kids never wonder about it
test: Mila's mate: «a letter once a year? that's nothing». What does it mean to Mila? | A lot; she keeps every one safe | Nothing; she throws them away | It's just boring junk mail to her, like adverts | She's never read any of them
test: Zac: «Gotcha day! pizza time!!» What are they celebrating? | The day his adoption became official | His birthday, the day he was born | The day he first caught a fish at the lake with Dad | The end of the school term
test: ★★★ Sam-Jo was 3 and Ruby was 6 when they were adopted. Sam-Jo is 9 now. How old is Ruby, and how many years ago were they adopted? | 12, and 6 years ago | 12, and 3 years ago | 15, and 6 years ago | 9, and 6 years ago
test: ★★★ Mila got her first yearly letter when she was 2. She is 10 now and got this year's letter. How many letters are in her box? | 9 | 8 | 10 | 2
test: Romilly calls her teddy «Judge». Why might she remember that day for ever? | It's the day her family became official | It's the day she got her sparkly shoes | It's the day she first met her grandad | It's the day she won a court case
test: «ur mum's well different-looking from u, are u adopted or summat?» What may Ana answer? | Whatever she wants, or nothing at all | Only the full story with all the details | She has to show proof of adoption | She must say she's not adopted
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises adoption? | The permanent joining of a child to a new family where the birth parents cannot raise the child | A temporary arrangement in which a child resides with carers for a short period only | An annual visit to relatives undertaken during the summer holidays by the child | The enrolment of a child at a residential school distant from the family home
exam: Identify the authority by which an adoption is rendered official. | A judge sitting in a family court | The head teacher of the child's school | The birth parents' local council | A notice published in a newspaper
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning letterbox contact. | Correspondence with the birth family is conveyed through an agency | Daily communication with birth parents by mobile telephone | The delivery of all adoption papers to a child's letterbox by the court | The prohibition of any correspondence with the birth family
exam: Given that an adopted child expresses curiosity concerning the birth family, ascertain the correct interpretation. | Such curiosity is normal and does not diminish love for the adoptive family | Such curiosity indicates that the child no longer loves the adoptive family | Such curiosity is forbidden by law until the child attains the age of thirty | Such curiosity indicates that the adoption must now be reversed by a court
